# The falcons' character classes

v1's QUOTE CHECK reads characters through a table of its own (`tpc_falcon` 0.2.0, `ucls`),
generated from Python's `unicodedata` at Unicode 15.1.0 and embedded in both of v1's
residences as text: items `<first>[-<last>]<class>`, code points in upper-case
hexadecimal, separated by `;`, in increasing order. The classes are L letter (Lu, Ll, Lt,
Lm, Lo), M mark (Mn, Mc, Me), D decimal digit (Nd), N other number (Nl, No), P dash (Pd),
S math symbol (Sm), C currency symbol (Sc), Z space separator (Zs) and F format (Cf); any
other code point has no class.

The table is ported as v1's data, character for character (`tableText`). The vector
runner compares it with the text in v1's source and checks that it reads into 1247
ranges in increasing order. v1 checked the text's SHA-256 against a stated digest when
it loaded it; the runner checks it with the Calculator's SHA-256 (`Principia.Sha256`).
When the table was ported, regenerating it from Python's `unicodedata`
15.1.0 by the rule above gave the same text.
-/

namespace Principia.Falcon

/-- A character's class in v1's table. -/
inductive CharClass where
  /-- L: a letter (Lu, Ll, Lt, Lm, Lo). -/
  | letter
  /-- M: a mark (Mn, Mc, Me). -/
  | mark
  /-- D: a decimal digit (Nd). -/
  | digit
  /-- N: another number (Nl, No). -/
  | number
  /-- P: a dash (Pd). -/
  | dash
  /-- S: a math symbol (Sm). -/
  | math
  /-- C: a currency symbol (Sc). -/
  | currency
  /-- Z: a space separator (Zs). -/
  | space
  /-- F: a format character (Cf). -/
  | format
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- The class a table item's last letter names. -/
def CharClass.ofCode : Char → Option CharClass
  | 'L' => some .letter
  | 'M' => some .mark
  | 'D' => some .digit
  | 'N' => some .number
  | 'P' => some .dash
  | 'S' => some .math
  | 'C' => some .currency
  | 'Z' => some .space
  | 'F' => some .format
  | _ => none

/-- The letter v1 writes for a class. -/
def CharClass.code : CharClass → Char
  | .letter => 'L'
  | .mark => 'M'
  | .digit => 'D'
  | .number => 'N'
  | .dash => 'P'
  | .math => 'S'
  | .currency => 'C'
  | .space => 'Z'
  | .format => 'F'

theorem CharClass.ofCode_code (c : CharClass) : CharClass.ofCode c.code = some c := by
  cases c <;> rfl

